{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789244845,"nonce":"oi/ngDE2zb8qznZltKJoa+215pA=","title":"Optimised tier never ran, and loops compiled before they executed keep generic call sites","message":"Two findings from the method_call investigation:\n\n1. beadie's broker only accepts beads in the Interpreted state, so every Optimized-tier submission was rejected (submit-rejected ... AlreadyQueued); tier stats showed opt=0 everywhere. The optimised compile now runs on a runtime thread and lands through the normal poll and swap path. Its compile is slow: 35-200 ms for the method_call module, because OSR variants were compiled for every edge to a lower block id (the inliner's continuation blocks made forward edges look backward) and for the headers of the inliner's generic loop copies. Targets now come from dominators and copies are excluded (osr_excluded); the compile is 35 ms for main plus two variants, still slow for a 368-instruction function.\n\n2. A loop that has not executed when its function compiles gets generic call sites forever (method_call's second loop: 17 ns per call). Implemented but gated off by WLIFT_COLD_LOOP_RECOMPILE=1: such headers get no OSR entry; OSR-entered code counts iterations at them and after 256 hands the header's live-ins (in the bytecode's own register numbering, restored as (register, value) pairs) back to the interpreter via wren_osr_exit and the UNDEFINED sentinel; the interpreter continues at the header, and the probe path requests the optimised recompile every 256 probes. It works (loop 2 is re-entered with warm caches at 70 ms) but loses on short loops because interpreting during the 35 ms recompile costs more than the generic calls: method_call 34 -\u003e 61 ms. It pays off once compile latency drops or the interpreter-to-compiled dispatch (now ~65 ns per call) gets cheaper, which is when it should be turned on.","files":null}]}